Interview Questions (Module 1)

Q: What is Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)?
A: EDA is the process of investigating a dataset’s main characteristics (distributions, relationships, anomalies) before formal modeling. 
   It uses summary statistics and visualizations to “gain useful insights” such as hidden trends or data quality issues.

Q: How do you handle missing data?
A: Common approaches are deleting rows/columns with too many missing values or imputing with substitutes (mean, median, mode, or model-based). 
   The method depends on the context (MCAR vs not at random) and preserving as much useful data as possible.

Q: Why is identifying outliers important, and how would you do it?
A: Outliers can distort analysis (e.g., mean, correlation). We detect them using statistical rules (e.g. values beyond 3σ from the mean, or outside 1.5×IQR) or visual tools (boxplots). 
   Treatment depends on cause: correct/remove erroneous ones, or use robust methods if they are valid extreme cases.

Q: When would you create new features during data understanding?
A: New features are created when they help reveal patterns (e.g., extracting “month” from a date to capture seasonality). 
   In EDA we do feature engineering (binning, encoding, transformations) to help models later.

Q: How can you detect data quality issues?
A: By calculating frequencies and summaries. For example, generating histograms may show impossible values (negative ages).
   Counting unique values can reveal unexpected categories. One can also join tables and look for mismatches.

End-of-Module Project:
Retail Sales Analysis. Using a provided sales dataset (with transactions, product and customer info), perform a full EDA: 
    clean the data (handle missing values, fix types), explore variable distributions, compute summary tables (e.g. revenue by category via Excel pivot), and document key findings. 
    For instance, identify trends (e.g. top-selling products, seasonal peaks) and any data issues.
